true] [17592186343502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343505 13194139535317 true] [17592186343502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343506 13194139535317 true] [17592186343502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343507 13194139535317 true] [17592186343502 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535317 true] [17592186343502 1041 "selection" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343502 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343507 13194139535317 true] [17592186343502 1110 #uuid "a9e24f85-4f60-59a2-a62f-d06551a574c1" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343503 1034 "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(250-M)" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535317 true] [17592186343503 1111 #uuid "8407ad99-313c-5377-b55a-af8d9d5a4410" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343504 1034 "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(50-M)" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535317 true] [17592186343504 1111 #uuid "c5c0d5c7-904e-55de-bc6a-86adcd1a8e27" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343505 1034 "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(35-M)" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535317 true] [17592186343505 1111 #uuid "22ef0aed-1bf5-52c0-8617-a94d57f8831e" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343506 1034 "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(150-M)" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535317 true] [17592186343506 1111 #uuid "478a8e5b-5aac-5e7a-9f61-2854217818d2" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343507 1034 "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535317 true] [17592186343507 1111 #uuid "7aa603dd-bf13-5e80-9bb9-9f609177d741" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343509 13194139535317 true] [17592186343508 1089 "q-335542" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535317 true] [17592186343508 1100 "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343508 1113 #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343508 1145 "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343510 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343511 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343512 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343513 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343514 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1041 "selection" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343510 13194139535317 true] [17592186343509 1110 #uuid "eb9baf1d-441e-5af4-9d80-374142fbfe66" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343510 1034 "$35$ days" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343510 1111 #uuid "22fe654c-ad21-50a8-ac11-f71dd2362418" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343511 1034 "$50$ days" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343511 1111 #uuid "4572b720-d1b9-5d0d-9e9a-a526040e9451" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343512 1034 "$40$ days" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343512 1111 #uuid "97d603fc-4a11-5a00-9589-a6bccd24c452" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343513 1034 "$20$ days" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343513 1111 #uuid "95eda9f5-5966-582f-b60b-33530d898344" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343514 1034 "$28$ days" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343514 1111 #uuid "b34e8b52-c664-5ead-aeec-501da362a9ca" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343515 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343516 13194139535317 true] [17592186343515 1089 "q-335578" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343515 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535317 true] [17592186343515 1100 "Throughout an experiment in a laboratory, a culture contains $1\\,200$ bacteria in total (limited by nutrients and space). At the beginning of the experiment, $200$ of the $1\\,200$ bacteria are resistant to a new antibiotic. After $4$ hours, the number of resistant bacteria increases to $500.$ To the nearest hour, how long does it take for the number of resistant bacteria to reach $900?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of resistant bacteria, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in hours.*" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343515 1113 #uuid "7ba1e4fc-1dbc-5357-89cb-5689e9814417" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343515 1145 "The rate at which the resistant bacteria population, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total bacteria population in the culture and its current resistant population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total bacteria population the culture can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,200$ bacteria, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,200-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,200-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,200-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,200-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial population of resistant bacteria was $200$ cells. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=200,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K{e}^{0} & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K & =200 \\\\ K & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $4$ hours, the resistant bacteria population was $500$ cells. So, applying the condition $P(4)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-4k} & =500 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,000{e}^{-4k} \\\\ {e}^{-4k} & =\\frac{700}{1\\,000}=\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $900$ bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =900 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =900 \\\\ 300 & =1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{300}{1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $900$ bacteria will be resistant to the antibiotic after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})}{-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})}=13.502…≈14\\,\\text{hours},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest hour." 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343517 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343518 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343519 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343520 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343521 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1041 "selection" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343521 13194139535317 true] [17592186343516 1110 #uuid "bc7c5879-ac6a-5ea6-8223-5c04dc13d7a9" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343517 1034 "$20$ hours" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343517 1111 #uuid "65a1c288-c2fa-5e43-b19a-85514233d1c1" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343518 1034 "$18$ hours" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343518 1111 #uuid "2d5caa66-af83-57c7-9e79-833e7f36022f" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343519 1034 "$10$ hours" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343519 1111 #uuid "0f81bfe1-5f56-5b56-8de8-42d7334abe06" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343520 1034 "$8$ hours" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343520 1111 #uuid "0ee06ded-77e7-53db-8b78-e9c654c08111" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343521 1034 "$14$ hours" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535317 true] [17592186343521 1111 #uuid "e8ae408f-10fa-50c0-91aa-29e3160455c3" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343522 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343523 13194139535317 true] [17592186343522 1089 "q-344214" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343522 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535317 true] [17592186343522 1100 "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $100$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139535317 true] [17592186343522 1113 #uuid "18c8891b-b109-5e81-a1d7-36a60599dfe8" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343522 1145 "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343524 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343525 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343526 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343527 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343528 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1041 "selection" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343526 13194139535317 true] [17592186343523 1110 #uuid "0b3fa011-08ec-508c-9e2b-f2e3c7d6d2ec" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343524 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k{F}^{2}(100-F)" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535317 true] [17592186343524 1111 #uuid "75442459-262b-53ca-a974-1a6004cbf8db" 13194139535317 true] [17592186343525 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100+F)" 1319